options.explanation:
Saying "$y$ is an implicit function of $x$" means the same as saying that $y(x)$ is a function whose equation is unknown.

The derivative of $y(x)$ is $y'(x)$, or $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.

We treat any expression that contains $y$ as a composite function, so differentiating it involves the chain rule.

For example, this is how we differentiate the term $y^3$ (from our equation) implicitly:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{d}{dx}(y^3)&=\dfrac{d}{dx}[y(x)]^3&&\gray{\text{Write }y\text{ as }y(x)}
\\\\
&=3[y(x)]^2\cdot y'(x)&&\gray{\text{Chain rule}}
\\\\
&=3y^2\cdot\dfrac{dy}{dx}&&\gray{\text{Go back to }y\text{ and }\dfrac{dy}{dx}}
\end{align}$

hints.content:
Now we can plug the point $(1,2)$ into the expression for $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$.

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{dy}{dx}&=\dfrac{3y-2x}{3y^2-3x}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{3(2)-2(1)}{3(2)^2-3(1)}\qquad\gray{x=1,\,\,y=2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{4}{9}
\end{align}$
